een Li/Zhang and Sun/Zhao.\n\nSo the pairs that are non-attendees in both cases would be:\n\n- Wang and Li: In Case 1, both non-attending.In Case 2, Wang is non-attending, Li is attending.So B is possible in Case 1 but not in Case 2.The answer is not C.</think>.\n\nThe answer is C) Li and Zhao.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**.\n\n1. **Case 1: Zhao attends.**.\n   - From constraint 1: Zhang must not attend.\n   - From constraint 2: Li must not attend.\n   - From constraint 3: At least one person between Zhang and Wang. Since Zhang is not attending, Wang can attend or not, but since we\'re in a case where Zhao attends, we\'ll see.\n   - From constraint 4: Two of Zhang, Sun, Zhou must attend. Since Zhang is not attending, Sun and Zhou must attend.\n   - From constraint 5: Wang and Li must both attend or neither. Since Li is not attending, Wang must not attend.\n   - From constraint 6: If Zhao doesn\'t attend, Sun doesn\'t attend. But in this case, Zhao is attending, so this doesn\'t affect Sun.\n   - Attendees: Zhao, Sun, Zhou.\n   - Non-attendees: Wang, Li, Zhang.\n\n2. **Case 2: Zhao does not attend.**.\n   - From constraint 1: Zhang must attend.\n   - From constraint 2: Li must attend.\n   - From constraint 3: At least one person between Zhang and Wang. Since Zhang is attending, Wang must not attend.\n   - From constraint 4: Two of Zhang, Sun, Zhou must attend. Since Zhang is attending, Sun and Zhou must attend.\n   - From constraint 5: Wang and Li must both attend or neither. Since Wang is not attending, Li must not attend. But this contradicts constraint 2, so this case is invalid.\n   - Therefore, Zhao must attend, and the attendees are Zhao, Sun, Zhou.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**.\n   - In both valid cases, Li and Zhao cannot both attend because:.\n     - In Case 1, Zhao attends, so Li cannot attend.\n     - In Case 2, the case is invalid because it leads to a contradiction.\n   - Therefore, Li and Zhao will not attend together. Therefore, my final option is: ']]  8