messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nParents who consistently laud their children for every attempt to accomplish something, whether successful or not, actually erode the youngsters' sense of self-esteem. Children require commendation for their achievements, but if uniformly praised for both what they have accomplished and what they have merely attempted, they will eventually discount all words of commendation. In effect, such children never hear any praise at all.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following most accurately expresses the overall conclusion of the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Children will develop low self-esteem if their parents do not praise them when they succeed.\nB) Children's self-esteem will suffer if their parents uniformly praise their attempts to accomplish things regardless of their success or failure.\nC) Parents should praise their children for their achievements.\nD) Children whose actions are praised undeservedly eventually learn to discount all words of praise.", "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this logical reasoning question.Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context says that parents who consistently praise their children for every attempt, whether successful or not, actually erode the kids' self-esteem.It mentions that children require commendation for their achievements, but if they're uniformly praised for both their successes and their attempts, they'll eventually discount all words of commendation.So, in effect, they never hear any praise at all.\n\nThe question is asking which option accurately expresses the overall conclusion of the argument.The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down the context again to make sure I understand it.The key points are:\n\n1.Parents who praise every attempt, both successful and unsuccessful, undermine the child's self-esteem.\n2.Children need commendation for achievements, but if they're praised for both achievements and attempts, they'll discount all praise.\n3.This means the children effectively don't hear any praise because it's all diluted.\n\nSo the main conclusion is about the negative effect of excessive praise on children's self-esteem.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) Children will develop low self-esteem if their parents do not praise them when they succeed.\n\nHmm, the context says that when parents praise every attempt, whether successful or not, it's bad.It doesn't specifically say about not praising when they succeed.So A might not be the best answer because the context doesn't talk about not praising successes, just about consistently praising all attempts.\n\nB) Children's self-esteem will suffer if their parents uniformly praise their attempts to accomplish things regardless of their success or failure.\n\nThis seems to align with the context.The parents are praised for every attempt, leading to low self-esteem.So B looks like a strong candidate.\n\nC) Parents should praise their children for their achievements.The context suggests that consistent praise for attempts boosts self-esteem, so B is likely correct as it aligns with the context.</think>.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
