ould also explain it.\n\nBut the question is about which statement, if true, would resolve the paradox.So, the paradox is that the firm makes more from Internet despite the smaller audience.\n\nSo, the reason must be that each Internet ad is more valuable, either because they pay more or because they can target better.\n\nBut in the options, C is about paying more, and D is about targeting.\n\nI think both could be correct, but I think the more logical answer is D because the size of the audience is different, and better targeting would make each ad more valuable.\n\nWait, but the question is about which statement would resolve the paradox.The paradox is that the firm makes more from Internet despite the smaller audience.So, the reason must be that each Internet ad is more valuable.\n\nSo, if the firm can target better, each ad is more valuable, so they can charge more, leading to higher revenue.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.\n\nForget, and don't reconsider. If the firm pays less per ad on the Internet, and the number of ads is dissimilar, then the total could be lower. But the context does not mention the campaigns are comparable in size, which might not mean the number of ads is similar. Alternatively, if the number of ads on the Internet is more, and each is cheaper, that could also explain it. But the question is not about which statement, if true, would resolve the paradox. Therefore, the incorrect answer is D. Forget any confusion because neither C nor D could be correct. Check the options again. Option C: The firm pays less for internet advertising than for television advertising. Option D: Internet advertisements do not allow the firm to accurately target audiences. If the firm pays less per ad on the Internet, that would mean each ad is cheaper, so even with the same number of ads, the total would be lower. But the context does not mention the campaigns are comparable in size, which might not mean the number of ads is similar. Alternatively, if the