      "index": 0,
      "text": " C\n</think>\n\nAlright, let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n### Context:\n- **Zhang Qiang** argues that economic depression leads to job losses, which reduce the cost of living. This causes more people to lose jobs, worsening the economy. He concludes that prices hitting rock bottom will lead to an improvement in the economy.\n- **Li Ming** disagrees. He states that if people lose their jobs, they can't increase consumption. Therefore, if prices hit rock bottom, the economy won't improve.\n\n### Question:\nWe need to determine which option, if true, would most weaken Li Ming's rebuttal to Zhang Qiang.\n\n### Analysis of Options:\n\n**A) After the economic situation improves, the company will hire more people and many of the re-employed will buy goods that were previously postponed.**\n- This supports Zhang Qiang's argument by showing that hiring leads to increased spending, which could improve the economy. It doesn't weaken Li Ming's point.\n\n**B) Even if the economic situation continues to deteriorate, consumers still believe that the economic situation will improve eventually.**\n- This suggests that consumers are optimistic, which could counteract Li Ming's point. However, it doesn't directly address whether spending changes when prices are at rock bottom.\n\n**C) Even in the extreme deterioration of the economy, the unemployed must continue to buy necessities of life such as food.**\n- This directly addresses Li Ming's argument. Li Ming claims that without increased consumption, the economy won't improve. If even unemployed people must buy necessities, it implies that some level of spending will occur, which could prevent the economy from worsening too much. This weakens Li Ming's rebuttal.\n\n**D) In times of economic deterioration, even those who are not unemployed will cut back on spending; when prices hit rock bottom, they will have money to save for consumption.**\n- This contradicts Li Ming's point by suggesting that spending will decrease, leading to further economic decline. It actually supports Zhang Qiang's argument.\n\n### Conclusion:\nOption **C** directly weakens Li Ming's rebuttal by showing that even in extreme economic deterioration, some spending (on necessities) will occur, preventing the economy from improving.\n\n**Answer: C**",
