- If X is in PRO, W does **not** need to be in PRO (Condition 3 only applies if X is in SALES).\n  - Additionally, since F is in PRO, and F and G cannot be in the same department, G must be in SALES or PR.\n  - If W were in PR, then since H and Y must be together, they could be in PRO or SALES.\n  - However, if W is in PR, then the remaining spots in PRO would need to be filled by F and two others, but F is already in PRO, so only two more can be in PRO. But since F is already in PRO, and G cannot be in PRO, G must be in SALES or PR.\n  - If W is in PR, then the remaining spots in PRO would be F and two others, but since F is already in PRO, only two more can be in PRO. However, since H and Y must be together, they can be in PRO or SALES.\n  - If H and Y are in PRO, then PRO would have F, H, Y, and W in PR, which would make PRO have 4 people, which is not possible since PRO only needs 3.\n  - Therefore, W cannot be in PR without violating the department size constraints.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe statement that **cannot be true** is:\n\n\\boxed{D}",
