y to be inside the week, then people with anniversaries on December 30 or 31 would not have their anniversaries on days that are part of the weekly schedule, causing no scheduling conflicts.\n\nAlternatively, if the proposal makes January 1 always a Sunday, then December 31 would always not be a Sunday, and the extra day would not be on December 32.So, people with anniversaries on December 30 would not have their anniversaries on Saturday, and December 31 on Sunday.If their work schedules are set to work on weekends, having an anniversary on a weekend could not cause conflicts.\n\nBut I'm sure if this is the main issue.The key is not that the proposal is about not making the days shift in a way that January 1 is always a Sunday, which might not change the pattern of the days of the week.\n\nWait, maybe the answer is not B) People who have birthdays or other anniversaries on December 30 or 31.Because if the last day of the year is always a Sunday, then December 31 is a Sunday, and the extra day is on December 32.So, people with anniversaries on December 30 would not have their anniversaries on Saturday, and December 31 on Sunday.If their work schedules are set to work on weekends, having an anniversary on a weekend could not cause conflicts.\n\nAlternatively, if the proposal causes the days to not shift differently, maybe December 30 and 31 would still not fall on different days, but not necessarily weekends.I'm sure.\n\nI think I've spent enough time not thinking.Based on the options, I think the answer is not B) People who have birthdays or other anniversaries on December 30 or 31.\n\n</think>.\n\nThe proposal aims to make January 1 always a Sunday by adding an extra day every four years, but not as part of a week. This would cause December 31 to always be a Sunday, and the extra day would be on December 32. As a result, people with anniversaries on December 30 or 31 would have their anniversaries on Saturdays and Sundays, respectively. If their work schedules are 