messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nWe ought to pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art. Its other, extrinsic properties are irrelevant to our aesthetic interactions with it. For example, when we look at a painting we should consider only what is directly presented in our experience of it. What is really aesthetically relevant, ttherefore, is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is added to the premises?\n# Options:\nA) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.\nB) Only an artwork's intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.\nC) It is possible in theory for an artwork to symbolize nothing.\nD) What an artwork symbolizes involves only extrinsic properties of that work.", "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem.Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that we should pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art.Extrinsic properties are irrelevant for our aesthetic interactions.For example, when looking at a painting, we should consider only what's directly presented, not what it symbolizes.So, the conclusion is that what's really aesthetically relevant isn't the symbolism but what's directly shown.\n\nThe question is asking which option, when added to the premises, would make the conclusion follow logically.The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down the premises first.The premises state that aesthetic interactions focus only on intrinsic properties.Extrinsics are irrelevant.So, the conclusion is that symbolism (which is extrinsic) isn't relevant; only what's directly presented matters.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) Th